From d6575af27218f2b564bb1b6fd9337c6b66a304a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:49:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10110/11362] HEAD-anchored seals and cross-scope unseal, for a
 standing declaration

Measuring what the conductor actually commits killed the obvious design. It
has AUTHORED changes to scripts/adversarial_verify.py 90 times, to
.pre-commit-config.yaml 9 times and to tests/python/conftest.py 3 times --
extending the fabrication gate is normal task work here, not an anomaly. So
sealing the harness against the conductor as first written would have refused
its commits routinely and wedged the research loop.

--seal-anchor head compares the working tree to HEAD instead of to a hash
captured at declaration time. A declaration that outlives many commits would
otherwise go stale the first time anyone legitimately committed a harness
change, then refuse every commit until a human noticed. Anchored to HEAD, a
committed change moves the baseline with it and only UNCOMMITTED harness edits
refuse -- which is the actual harm: an in-flight edit swept into someone else's
commit, changing the harness unreviewed and misattributed.

An explicit --unseal in ANY active declaration lifts that path for the commit.
Without it a standing seal deadlocks whoever is repairing the file it seals:
their own --unseal cannot reach another declaration's seal, so they would have
to release someone else's scope to commit -- the exact move the refusal text
tells them not to make. This does permit bypassing a standing seal by naming
the path, deliberately: a glob still never unseals, so the bypass costs a typed
path name and leaves the intent in the record.

Mutation-proved: 13 of 13 rules RED when deleted, guard restored
byte-identical, suite green before and after. 31 tests.

REQ-INFRA-6801.

